\section{What Are NDAs?}
An NDA is a legally binding agreement that prohibits certain individuals from disclosing confidential business information to others. These agreements are common during mergers, acquisitions, or when sharing proprietary technology.

\subsection{What an NDA Actually Contains}
An NDA typically covers:
• What information is protected (trade secrets, client lists, pricing data)
• Who has access (employees, contractors, partners)
• Restrictions on disclosure (time limits, scope of use)
• Consequences for breach (civil or criminal remedies)
• Termination conditions

\subsection{Does an NDA Cover All Information?}
No. An NDA protects specific trade secrets, but it does not prohibit everything. It also protects personal information that is already public (company website, LinkedIn profile, public press releases). Most employment contracts already contain confidentiality clauses, but an NDA adds additional protections for non-public trade secrets.

\subsection{Should You Sign One?}
If you are considering signing an NDA:
• Read the entire document before signing
• Ask for a copy of the entire agreement
• Verify what you should disclose versus what belongs to the company
• If you have doubts, consult an attorney

\section{What You Should Know About NDAs}
Before signing, ask these questions:
• What happens if I disclose something inadvertently?
• Can I discuss the company as a potential employer?
